{"reference":"1 Kings 22","book":"1 Kings","chapter":22,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The conflict between Ahab and Micaiah brings First Kings’ larger movement into focus: the book has moved from disputed succession through Solomon’s wisdom, temple, and failure, into divided kingdoms and prophetic confrontation under Ahab. This chapter therefore closes Ahab’s story with a direct clash between royal plans and prophetic speech.","verse_refs":["1 Kings 22:1-40"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_022_1407b7aaa191cfdf5dae4751"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Ahab in the Wider Setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"An inscription known as the Kurkh Monolith names Ahab in a coalition at Qarqar, an event not narrated in First Kings. It independently places an Israelite king in ninth-century regional politics, though it does not establish every detail or theological claim made in the book.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_022_990ace87214c10fe3fcc9759"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}